# The Thirsty Crow — modeled in ThoughtML.
#
# The moral is a claim just like any other — one supported by what
# actually happened in the story, not authored from outside. The crow's
# "decision" is its *inference* (`infers`) plus its *rejection* (`rejects`)
# of the obvious alternative. We timestamp every event so an `--as-of`
# replay can ask "what was true at minute N?"

scope the-fable-of-the-thirsty-crow
  source aesop

  # --- the events, in order ---

  observation crow-is-thirsty
    A thirsty crow wanders on a hot day looking for water.
    observed-at 2026-07-13T10:00

  observation finds-pitcher
    Crow finds a pitcher with a little water at the bottom.
    observed-at 2026-07-13T10:01

  observation water-too-low
    The water level sits below the reach of the crow's beak.
    observed-at 2026-07-13T10:02

  observation notices-stones
    Crow notices small pebbles on the ground beside the pitcher.
    observed-at 2026-07-13T10:03

  action drops-stones
    Crow drops stones into the pitcher one by one.
    observed-at 2026-07-13T10:04

  observation water-rises
    The water level rises as the stones displace it.
    observed-at 2026-07-13T10:14

  outcome crow-drinks
    Crow drinks the water and flies away refreshed.
    observed-at 2026-07-13T10:15

  # --- the causal chain (structural, not evidence — no polarity) ---

  link water-too-low  enables notices-stones
  link notices-stones enables drops-stones
  link drops-stones  causes   water-rises
  link water-rises   enables  crow-drinks

  # --- the crow's reasoning ---

  # The crow's inference: the four pieces compose into a workable plan.
  # `infers` auto-wires each source as a `supports` edge to the plan.
  crow infers plan-works from finds-pitcher notices-stones drops-stones water-rises
    confidence 0.9 assumed
    because the four pieces compose into a workable plan

  # The crow considered the obvious alternative and rejected it.
  crow considers give-up
  crow rejects give-up
    confidence 0.95 assumed
    because the pitcher is right there

  # --- the moral ---

  claim moral-necessity-drives-invention
    Necessity is the mother of invention.

  # The moral is supported by what actually happened in the story.
  supports moral-necessity-drives-invention
    plan-works  weight 0.9 measured
    crow-drinks weight 0.8 measured

  storyteller remembers moral-necessity-drives-invention
    confidence 0.95 measured
    because the plan worked the first time
